\# AI Sommelier Architecture V3



\## 1. Purpose



The Brewcipe AI Coffee Sommelier provides conversational coffee recommendations grounded in Brewcipe's canonical structured data.



The LLM acts as a recommendation and conversational interface. It does not replace Brewcipe's database as the canonical source of recipe information.



\---



\## 2. LLM Provider



\*\*Provider:\*\* Google Gemini



\*\*SDK:\*\* Google GenAI SDK (`google-genai`)



The Gemini API is called only from the Brewcipe backend.



API credentials must remain server-side and must never be exposed to the frontend.



\---



\## 3. High-Level Flow



```text

User

&#x20; ↓

AI Sommelier interface

&#x20; ↓

FastAPI backend

&#x20; ↓

Retrieve relevant Brewcipe recipe candidates

&#x20; ↓

Format limited structured context

&#x20; ↓

Send user question + Brewcipe context to Gemini

&#x20; ↓

Gemini recommends several suitable recipes

&#x20; ↓

Backend validates recommendation identifiers

&#x20; ↓

Frontend displays recommendations

&#x20; ↓

User can:

\- choose a recipe

\- request another set of suggestions

```



\---



\## 3A. Personalized Sommelier Context

For authenticated users, the Sommelier may use the current personalization context built by the backend:

```text
Taste Profile
     +
Tried Recipes
     +
Skipped Recipes
     +
Relevant Brewcipe Candidates
     ↓
Controlled Gemini Request
```

The taste profile is the primary preference signal. Tried recipes provide evidence of what the user has explored or enjoyed, while skipped recipes provide avoidance evidence.

Skipped recipe IDs are excluded from the Sommelier candidate pool before candidates are supplied to Gemini.

The Sommelier uses this context to personalize conversational recommendations; Gemini does not own or persist the canonical taste model.

## 4. Recommendation Boundaries



\* The AI Sommelier may recommend only existing Brewcipe coffee records when presenting Brewcipe-specific recommendations.

\* The AI must not invent coffee IDs, recipe IDs, recipe names, ingredients, instructions, geography, or source attribution.

\* Brewcipe's structured database remains the canonical source for recipe facts.

\* The LLM may explain why a Brewcipe record matches the user's preferences.

\* If there is no strong Brewcipe match, the AI should say so rather than inventing one.

\* The AI may offer the closest available Brewcipe matches when appropriate.

\* General coffee advice may be conversational, but Brewcipe-specific factual claims must come from backend-provided context.

\* Only a limited set of relevant Brewcipe records should be sent to Gemini, not the full production dataset.



\---



\## 4A. Personalization Rules

The Sommelier should:

* use the user's taste profile as the primary preference signal
* treat tried recipes as evidence of prior exploration or enjoyment
* treat skipped recipes as avoidance evidence
* use interaction history to refine recommendations when relevant
* recommend only from the backend-supplied candidate set

---

## 5. Grounding Strategy



\* Retrieve candidate recipes from Brewcipe's canonical database before calling Gemini.

\* Use structured Brewcipe metadata to select relevant candidates.

\* Limit the number of records supplied to Gemini.

\* Send only fields required for recommendation reasoning.

\* Treat retrieved Brewcipe records as the sole source of Brewcipe-specific facts.

\* Instruct Gemini to recommend only from the supplied candidate records.

\* Return several suitable Brewcipe recommendations rather than a single recommendation.

\* Present approximately three recommendations per response for the MVP.

\* Require every recommendation to reference an existing Brewcipe identifier.

\* Validate returned identifiers against the retrieved candidate set.

\* Allow users to select any recommended recipe.

\* Allow users to request another set of recommendations.

\* When generating another set, avoid previously shown recommendations where suitable alternatives exist.

\* Return an empty recommendation list when no suitable Brewcipe match exists.

\* Do not send the entire production dataset to the LLM.



\---



\## 6. Candidate Selection Strategy



1\. Receive the user's natural-language question.



2\. Use the question to identify useful preference signals such as:



&#x20;  \* geography: country, region, continent

&#x20;  \* serving temperature: hot / cold / iced

&#x20;  \* ingredients: milk, condensed milk, sugar, spices, etc.

&#x20;  \* brewer or preparation method

&#x20;  \* recipe type

&#x20;  \* meal/context preferences where relevant



3\. Retrieve a limited pool of Brewcipe recipes from the canonical database.



4\. Include structured metadata for those candidates:



&#x20;  \* `coffee\_recipe\_id`

&#x20;  \* `english\_name`

&#x20;  \* `native\_name`

&#x20;  \* `transliteration`

&#x20;  \* recipe type

&#x20;  \* countries / region / continent

&#x20;  \* brewers

&#x20;  \* serving temperatures

&#x20;  \* ingredient names

&#x20;  \* associated meal / meal description



5\. Do not include full recipe instructions or source data during recommendation selection unless they are specifically needed.



6\. Send only this limited candidate pool to Gemini.



7\. Gemini ranks the supplied candidates and recommends approximately three.



8\. Validate every returned `coffee\_recipe\_id` against the supplied candidate set.



9\. On \*\*Suggest Again\*\*, exclude previously shown IDs where enough alternatives remain.



\---



\## 7. AI Request Model



The frontend submits a natural-language recommendation request.



Example:



```json

{

&#x20; "message": "I want something sweet and milky from Southeast Asia"

}

```



The initial request model is:



```text

SommelierRequest

\- message: string

```



The message is limited to a reasonable maximum length to control usage and prevent unnecessarily large LLM requests.



\---



\## 8. AI Response Model



The backend returns conversational text together with grounded Brewcipe recommendations.



Example structure:



```json

{

&#x20; "answer": "Here are three Brewcipe coffees that fit what you're looking for.",

&#x20; "recommendations": \[

&#x20;   {

&#x20;     "coffee\_id": "existing-brewcipe-uuid",

&#x20;     "coffee\_recipe\_id": "existing-recipe-id",

&#x20;     "english\_name": "Coffee name",

&#x20;     "reason": "Why this recipe matches the user's preferences."

&#x20;   }

&#x20; ]

}

```



The response models are:



```text

SommelierResponse

\- answer: string

\- recommendations: SommelierRecommendationResponse\[]



SommelierRecommendationResponse

\- coffee\_id: UUID

\- coffee\_recipe\_id: string

\- english\_name: string

\- reason: string

```



The recommendation list may be empty when Brewcipe has no suitable match.



\---



\## 9. Context Minimisation



Gemini should receive only the information required to make a useful recommendation.



Recommendation context may include:



\* recipe identifier

\* coffee name

\* recipe type

\* geography

\* brewer

\* serving temperature

\* ingredient names

\* associated meal information where relevant



The following should normally remain outside the LLM request:



\* full recipe instructions

\* source records

\* unnecessary database identifiers

\* unrelated production records

\* the complete Brewcipe dataset



This reduces unnecessary production-data exposure, request size, cost, and hallucination risk.



\---



\## 10. Suggest Again Behaviour



The Sommelier should provide approximately three recommendations per response.



After receiving recommendations, the user may:



1\. choose a recommended recipe and navigate to its canonical Brewcipe recipe page, or

2\. request another set of recommendations.



When the user chooses \*\*Suggest Again\*\*:



\* the original preference should remain applicable;

\* previously shown recipes should be excluded where appropriate;

\* suitable alternative Brewcipe candidates should be preferred;

\* previously shown recipes may be reused only when there are insufficient relevant alternatives.



